\subsection{Degradation Under Inboard-Diesel Engine Noise}
\label{sec:engine-noise-degradation}

The sensor noise injected everywhere else in this work is electronic: white
noise, initial bias, bias random walk, and magnetometer calibration error.  A
cruising sailboat carrying this estimator spends a substantial fraction of its
passages under power, and an inboard auxiliary diesel puts mechanical
vibration into the same accelerometer and gyroscope.  This section measures
what that costs.

\subsubsection{Vibration model}

The simulator gains a sensor-path model of the archetypal auxiliary for a
\SIrange{35}{45}{ft} cruising boat: a naturally aspirated three-cylinder
four-stroke diesel on flexible mounts, a $2.6\!:\!1$ reduction gear, and a
three-blade fixed propeller.  It injects the crank orders --- the half order,
the first order, the firing order $n_{\rm cyl}/2$ and its harmonics ---
together with driveline shaft-rate and propeller-blade-rate lines, a broadband
structural floor above a few Hz, governor hunting and combustion variability
that widen each order from a line into a narrow band, accelerometer vibration
rectification, and gyroscope linear-acceleration sensitivity.  The vessel's
rigid-body response to the sea is untouched; only the sensor path changes.

Amplitudes are physical rather than fitted.  Each order is an inertial
excitation growing as $f^{2}$, transmitted through a mount of natural
frequency $f_n$ and damping $\zeta$ with
%
\begin{equation}
  T(f) = \sqrt{\frac{1 + (2\zeta r)^{2}}{(1-r^{2})^{2} + (2\zeta r)^{2}}},
  \qquad r = f/f_n ,
  \label{eq:mount-transmissibility}
\end{equation}
%
so a single gain, calibrated once so the hull broadband RMS matches a stated
level at a reference speed, fixes the level at every other speed.  The speed
dependence this produces is the familiar one rather than a plain $\rm rpm^{2}$
law: a diesel auxiliary is rough at idle, where the low orders sit near the
mount resonance and $T > 1$, and smooths relative to its excitation as the
orders climb past it.  The sensor is given a finite two-pole anti-alias
bandwidth ahead of the \SI{200}{Hz} sample rate, and because each line's phase
is advanced at that sample rate, orders above Nyquist fold on their own.

The nominal operating point is \SI{2400}{rpm} with \SI{0.60}{m.s^{-2}} of hull
broadband RMS and an \SI{80}{Hz} sensor bandwidth, of which
\SI{0.454}{m.s^{-2}} survives to the sample.  That level is about
\SI{0.061}{g}; at the \SIrange{30}{60}{Hz} carrying most of the energy it is
roughly \SI{2}{mm.s^{-1}} RMS velocity, inside the range ISO 6954 treats as
comfortable for small-craft accommodation.  It is a routine cruise condition
for a sensor on a cabin bulkhead, not a worst case, and the sweeps below run
from a quieter installation to a sensor near the engine bed.

\subsubsection{Cost at the nominal cruise condition}

Table~\ref{tab:engine-noise-cruise} replays the eight stationary records
through each family with the engine off and at the nominal cruise condition.
This is the comparison that motivates the remedy of
Sec.~\ref{sec:engine-noise-guard}, so it holds all three families in the same
unconditioned configuration: OU--III's front-end guard, which ships armed, is
switched off throughout this subsection and switched on again there.
All three degrade by a large factor.  OU--III's pooled 3-D displacement RMS
goes from \SI{0.522}{m} to \SI{4.232}{m}, a factor of \num{8.1}; vertical RMS
goes from \SI{0.179}{m} to \SI{0.828}{m}.  OU--II degrades by \num{5.2} and
TFG by \num{18.8}.

