  \centering
  \caption{Pooled error over eight stationary records with the engine off and
  at \SI{2400}{rpm}.  ``Offset'' is the systematic part: the RMS across
  records of each record's mean error.}
  \label{tab:engine-noise-cruise}
  \footnotesize
  \setlength{\tabcolsep}{3.2pt}
  \begin{tabular}{@{}llrrrr@{}}
    \toprule
    Family & Engine & 3-D [m] & 3-D offset [m] & Pitch [\si{\degree}] & Yaw [\si{\degree}] \\
    \midrule
    OU--II  & off  &  0.642 &  0.178 &  0.196 &  0.67 \\
    OU--II  & on   &  3.363 &  2.862 &  2.436 & 58.59 \\
    OU--III & off  &  0.522 &  0.140 &  0.158 &  0.63 \\
    OU--III & on   &  4.232 &  3.875 &  3.084 & 45.38 \\
    TFG     & off  &  0.745 &  0.206 &  0.386 &  0.80 \\
    TFG     & on   & 14.026 & 12.903 & 16.205 & 74.35 \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

The ordering is worth stating plainly: OU--III has the lowest engine-off
displacement error of the three, but at the cruise condition OU--II is lower
(\SI{3.363}{m} against \SI{4.232}{m}).  The margin OU--III holds over OU--II
in the clean case does not survive vibration.  What both OU families do retain
is their margin over TFG, whose error is more than three times OU--III's and
whose degradation factor is \num{18.8} against \num{8.1}.

\subsubsection{The error becomes an offset}

The RMS understates what has happened.  With the engine off, OU--III's
\SI{0.522}{m} 3-D error is mostly fluctuation: the systematic part is
\SI{0.140}{m}, \pct{27} of it.  At cruise the systematic part is
\SI{3.875}{m} of \SI{4.232}{m}, \pct{92}.  The attitude channel shows the same
thing more sharply still --- pitch RMS is \SI{3.084}{\degree} and the pitch
\emph{offset} is \SI{3.075}{\degree}, so the attitude error is a static tilt
with almost no fluctuation on it.  Vibration does not add noise to the
estimate; it moves the estimate's operating point.

This identifies the mechanism.  The accelerometer update is a joint
measurement of attitude and world acceleration, with predicted specific force
$\hat{f} = R_{wb}(\hat{a}_w - g)$ and a covariance sized for a quiet sensor.
Wide-band vibration cannot be attributed to $\hat{a}_w$, whose OU prior has
wave-band bandwidth, so the residual is taken up by attitude and by the
accelerometer bias state.  The resulting static tilt then leaks gravity into
the strapdown horizontal acceleration --- \SI{3}{\degree} of tilt is
\SI{0.51}{m.s^{-2}} --- which the integral-regularized displacement estimator
converts into the bounded static displacement offset that dominates the table.
Consistently, OU--III's accelerometer bias-state error rises from
\SI{0.046}{m.s^{-2}} with the engine off to \SI{0.469}{m.s^{-2}} at cruise.

Two candidate mechanisms are ruled out by the same evidence.  Modeled
vibration rectification contributes an offset of \SI{2.3e-5}{m.s^{-2}}, five
orders of magnitude below the bias-state error, so it is not the source.  And
rerunning the cruise cell with the model's gyroscope terms switched off leaves
every family unchanged to four significant figures (OU--III \SI{4.2318}{m}
against \SI{4.2316}{m}): the degradation is entirely an accelerometer-path
effect.

\subsubsection{Onset, and what controls it}

Fig.~\ref{fig:engine-noise-speed} sweeps engine speed.  Degradation is present
at idle and grows monotonically with the vibration the model transmits.  The
important feature is that there is no quiet regime to operate in: at
\SI{800}{rpm} OU--III already carries a \SI{2.014}{\degree} tilt offset
against \SI{0.144}{\degree} with the engine off, and even at the quietest
level swept --- \SI{0.15}{m.s^{-2}} of hull vibration, \SI{0.114}{m.s^{-2}}
recorded, a well-isolated installation --- the offset is
\SI{1.957}{\degree}, more than thirteen times the engine-off value.  The tilt
offset appears almost fully formed as soon as any vibration is present and
then grows slowly; what grows quickly with level is the displacement offset it
drives.

\IfFileExists{ou_engine_noise_speed.svg}{%
  \begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includesvg[width=\columnwidth,inkscapelatex=false]{ou_engine_noise_speed}
    \caption{Engine-speed sweep at the nominal vibration level.  The first
    panel is the injected vibration itself: hull RMS, and the part surviving
    the sensor's anti-alias bandwidth.  Dotted horizontals in the remaining
    panels are each family's engine-off baseline.  The hull level does not
    follow $\rm rpm^{2}$, because the low orders pass through the mount
    resonance at idle.}
    \label{fig:engine-noise-speed}
  \end{figure}
}{}

Fig.~\ref{fig:engine-noise-mechanism} separates level from spectrum.  The
level sweep shows a knee rather than a gentle trend: between \SI{0.60}{} and
\SI{1.20}{m.s^{-2}} of hull vibration OU--III goes from \num{8.1} to \num{72}
times its baseline, \SI{37.5}{m}, which is loss of lock rather than
degradation.  Narrowing the sensor's anti-alias bandwidth from \SI{80}{} to
\SI{20}{Hz} recovers most of the loss, taking OU--III from \SI{4.232}{m} to
\SI{0.791}{m}.

The matched-power control establishes why.  Repeating the bandwidth sweep with
the hull level rescaled so every cell records the same \SI{0.454}{m.s^{-2}}
leaves the folded line frequencies different from cell to cell but the
recorded power equal.  Across an \num{8}:1 bandwidth range the as-is cells
span a factor of \num{9.6} for OU--III and \num{9.4} for OU--II; the matched
cells span \num{2.0} and \num{1.9}.  Degradation is therefore set by how much
out-of-band accelerometer power survives to the sample, and not by where the
aliased orders happen to land --- including the cells where an order folds to
within a fraction of a Hz of DC, which does not stand out.  The practical
consequence is that anti-alias filtering and mechanical isolation act on the
quantity that matters, while choosing a cruising rpm to move the aliases does
not.

