    hull vibration level at cruise rpm, with engine-off baselines dotted.  Top
    right: the anti-alias bandwidth sweep (solid, filled) and the same sweep
    with the level rescaled to a common recorded power (dashed, open); the
    matched cells collapse where the as-is cells spread.  Bottom left: every
    cell of the study against recorded vibration RMS.  Bottom right: the
    static tilt offset that drives all of it, which is already an order of
    magnitude above the engine-off value (dotted) at the quietest level
    tested.}
    \label{fig:engine-noise-mechanism}
  \end{figure}
}{}

\subsubsection{A front-end guard, and what it recovers}
\label{sec:engine-noise-guard}

The attribution above is narrow enough to act on.  The damage is done by
out-of-band power in one sensor, its size follows how much of that power
survives to the sample, and the wave band it has to be separated from is a
decade below it.  Nothing about that requires the estimator to change: it
requires the vibration not to reach it.

\texttt{AccelVibrationGuard} is placed at the single point in
\texttt{updateCore\_} where raw measurements arrive, so the Mahony proxy, the
MEKF, and the tilt watchdog all read the same conditioned accelerometer and no
consumer can be left on a different version of it.  It is a two-pole cascade at
\SI{14}{Hz}, in the empty spectrum between the two bands.

The corner is chosen against delay rather than against rejection.  A low pass
costs group delay $\tau = n_{\rm poles}/(2\pi f_c)$, flat across the wave band,
and acceleration delayed by $\tau$ yields displacement delayed by $\tau$: an
error of $A \, 2\pi f \tau$ that grows with wave amplitude, so an
unconditional filter charges the biggest seas the most.  Two poles at
\SI{14}{Hz} cost \SI{22.7}{ms}.  Sweeping the alternatives, \SI{10}{Hz} costs
\SI{32}{ms} for no better result and \SI{20}{Hz} costs \SI{16}{ms} for a
markedly worse one; at matched delay a longer cascade rejects less of what
matters here, because the damaging content sits just above the corner rather
than deep in the stopband.

Because the delay is only worth paying when there is something to remove, the
guard engages on a measurement rather than always.  A separate two-pole
high-pass at \SI{25}{Hz} watches the accelerometer, and the guard ramps in over
\SI{5}{s} as that reading crosses \SIrange{0.03}{0.08}{m.s^{-2}}.  The detector
is deliberately placed well above the conditioning corner: at the corner it
would be reading the top of the sea spectrum, which grows with wave height, and
would then engage hardest in big seas, which is exactly backwards.  Placed
above the sea it reads \SIrange{0.00796}{0.00805}{m.s^{-2}} across the eight
stationary records --- a one percent spread over a \num{31}:1 range of $H_s$ ---
against \SI{0.087}{m.s^{-2}} at idle and \SI{0.144}{m.s^{-2}} at the nominal
cruise condition.

Conditioning alone already recovers most of the loss, but it leaves a residual,
and forcing the guard on over a \emph{quiet} input says what that residual is
made of.  On the JONSWAP records at $H_s = \SI{1.5}{m}$ and \SI{8.5}{m} the
delay alone costs \num{1.021} and \num{1.063} against deployed residuals of
\num{1.074} and \num{1.151}: roughly half the gap is the guard's own group
delay, which no covariance change can touch, and the other half is vibration
that survived the stopband.

That other half is measurement error the MEKF is not told about, so the second
stage tells it.  The commanded accelerometer standard deviation is raised to
$\sqrt{\sigma^{2} + (\kappa \, e)^{2}}$, where $e$ is the guard's detector
reading above its engagement floor --- the same gated excess, so this stage is
inert on a quiet installation for the same reason the guard is.

The gain is not free in both channels at once, which is what fixes it.
Attitude improves monotonically as the accelerometer is de-weighted, but the
accelerometer is also the only measurement of wave acceleration there is, so
past $\kappa \approx \num{1.25}$ the displacement estimate starts leaning on
the OU prior and the 3-D error turns back up.  Swept pooled over the eight
records, the displacement optimum is broad and flat between \num{0.75} and
\num{1.0}; the deployed value is $\kappa = \num{0.75}$, at the optimum with
margin below the cliff.

Table~\ref{tab:engine-noise-guard} is the result of both stages.  At the
nominal cruise condition pooled 3-D RMS falls from \SI{4.232}{m} unguarded to
\SI{0.596}{m} with conditioning and \SI{0.577}{m} with the covariance stage
as well, so the penalty against the engine-off baseline goes from a factor of
\num{8.1} to \num{1.10}.  The second stage earns most of its keep where the
first leaves most behind: at the engine bed it takes \SI{0.834}{m} down to
\SI{0.611}{m}, a residual of \num{1.17} against \num{1.60}, and near maximum
revs \SI{0.675}{m} to \SI{0.588}{m}.  The standing tilt offset it targets
falls from \SI{1.180}{\degree} to \SI{0.444}{\degree} at cruise and yaw from
\SI{4.17}{\degree} to \SI{1.84}{\degree}.

Both stages are driven by the detector and both release when the machinery
stops; nothing is latched.  Shutting the modeled engine down part-way through a
record measures it: from full engagement at \SI{2400}{rpm} the covariance
returns to its nominal value in \SI{11.5}{s} and the guard fully disengages,
giving back its group delay, in \SI{54}{s}.  The covariance releases first,
which is the safe order --- it stops understating the measurement while the low
pass is still settling rather than the reverse --- and the difference is
structural, since the covariance follows the detector directly while the
conditioning weight additionally slews on a \SI{5}{s} time constant so a
throttle change cannot step the measurement path.

One condition does not benefit.  At \SI{800}{rpm} the residual rises from
\num{1.145} to \num{1.153}, \pct{0.7}: idle puts the crank orders lowest in
frequency, so the guard removes least of them and what leaks through is closest
to the wave band, where de-weighting the accelerometer cannot separate it from
the sea.

