The engine-off row is the other half of the claim, and it is why the guard is
armed by default rather than offered as an option.  With no machinery running
the detector never reaches its lower rail, the guard returns its input
unchanged, and the replay is bit-identical to the unguarded one --- on all
eight stationary records, and on every record shipped with the simulation
archive.  There is therefore no quiet-water case to trade away: no fitted
quality gate is re-cut and no committed replay is invalidated by arming it.

The measurement above is what makes that safe, and it is a property of the
detector rather than a coincidence.  Because the detector sits well above the
sea, its clean reading is set by the accelerometer's own white noise: across a
\num{100}:1 range of wave amplitude it varies by \pct{0.02}.  A big sea cannot
look like machinery to it, and a near-still one cannot lower the bar.

What remains is the part neither stage can reach.  Group delay is the price of
conditioning and is still paid when there is nothing left to remove, so the
residual cannot go to one while the guard is engaged; and no front-end filter
helps with machinery whose orders reach into the wave band, because there is
nothing there to separate them from the sea.  The \SI{800}{rpm} row is that
limit showing itself early.

\subsubsection{Scope}

This is a sensor-path result and should not be read as the full cost of
motoring.  The magnetometer is left unperturbed, the wave records are the same
stationary seas used elsewhere, and the vessel's rigid-body motion does not
change: a real passage under power also alters the encounter spectrum, adds
propeller-induced surge, and runs the engine at a speed that itself varies
with the sea.  Nor is the estimator retuned for the condition.  The
measurement covariances, adaptation law, pseudo-measurements, startup logic,
and regularization are exactly those deployed, which is the point: the
numbers above are what the shipped configuration does when the boat starts its
engine.  What they indicate is that the remedy belongs in the measurement path
rather than in the wave model, which is where
Sec.~\ref{sec:engine-noise-guard} puts it.

